From 1c2614d8dcae30e9749ad753e1fa516a9c984b19 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 15:21:23 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 104/145] media: meson: vdec: hevc: allocate compressed bodies
 outside CMA

In MMU mode the compressed body is the largest allocation a session makes -
measured at 320 MiB for a 4K 10-bit stream, the biggest single draw on CMA
in the driver - and it is the one allocation that provably does not need to
be contiguous at all.  The hardware never strides it: it reaches
body pages only through the frame page map.  Four independent points in the
existing code say so.  codec_hevc_fill_mmu_map() builds a per-4 KiB-page PFN
table; codec_hevc_setup_decode_head() writes HEVCD_MPP_DECOMP_CTL2 = 0 in
MMU mode, deliberately neutering the stride register; meson_overlay.c sets
vd1_afbc_body_addr = 0 in scatter mode because the header carries the
layout; and the chunk pool this patch modifies already hands out 16
unrelated 1 MiB pieces per 4K frame and measures 61.98 dB PSNR - if the
hardware strode linearly, 15 of those 16 MiB would land outside the buffer.

So the body was already page-mapped.  It was simply being allocated in a
unit - 1 MiB, coherent - that only CMA can serve.  Allocate it a page at a
time instead: dma_alloc_contiguous() returns NULL for a PAGE_SIZE request
when the device has no per-device CMA area, so the allocation falls through
to the page allocator and never enters CMA.  Chunks stay 256 pages so the
pool's bookkeeping, and the generational parking rule that protects a body
the display may still be scanning out, are unchanged.

Three details that are not free choices:

  - dma_alloc_pages(), not alloc_pages().  The device runs on the default
    32-bit coherent mask; dma_alloc_pages() applies it and retries from a
    lower zone, which raw page allocation would skip.

  - The pages are cached, where the coherent range they replace was not.
    Nothing in this driver reads or writes a body through the CPU - only the
    decoder and the display touch it, at the point of coherency - so the
    cache needs handling exactly twice: invalidate after allocation, or the
    dirty lines dma_alloc_pages() leaves behind when it zeroes the page get
    written back over decoded data, and invalidate again before the page
    returns to the allocator, so no speculatively-loaded stale line outlives
    it.  Chunks moving through the pool need nothing in between.

  - __GFP_NORETRY.  An order-0 GFP_KERNEL allocation does not fail; it
    reclaims and then invokes the OOM killer.  A 4K session asks for ~65k of
    them, and a decode that cannot get memory has to fail cleanly - the
    caller already turns that into -ENOMEM - rather than kill the player to
    make room for itself.

The !use_mmu path is deliberately untouched: pre-G12A FBC really does stride
the body, and there the body stays one contiguous coherent range.

Measured on a Khadas VIM3 (A311D), one 4K Main10 HEVC session, low-water
mark sampled while the session is live rather than the residue afterwards:

                        before        after
  CmaFree           161,724 kB    489,404 kB     (+320 MiB exactly)
  CMA maxchunk       35,840 kB    117,768 kB
  MemFree         1,953,112 kB  1,939,852 kB     (unchanged)

MemFree holding still is the point: CMA pages count toward MemFree, so the
total demand is the same and exactly 320 MiB of it moved out of the
contended pool.

Output is unchanged.  Against a software decode with the same ffmpeg, both
builds score 47.281935 dB average / 41.056775 dB min on 4K Main10 HEVC,
54.153593 dB on 1080p Main10, and 54.153062 dB on 4K VP9 Profile 2 - equal
to six decimals - while 8-bit HEVC, VP9 and H.264 stay bit-exact 24/24.

Lifetime was checked separately, because no correctness test can see a
use-after-free that now lands in the page allocator rather than in CMA: 24
sessions across three codecs and both depths, including mid-stream
teardowns, then 2 GiB of file I/O to force reuse of every returned page, all
under debug_pagealloc=on slub_debug=FZP.  Zero kernel faults, zero driver
complaints, and CmaFree flat across cycles.

Signed-off-by: gus bourg <gus@bourg.net>
---
